    "description": "This is a task to identify the correct reasoning thoughts while solving a circuit topology inference problem from voltage and current measurements.",
    "examples": [
      {
        "input": "Given the following measurements in a 3-resistor circuit connected between nodes A and B:\n\n- Total current from source: 3A at 12V (A to B)\n- Current through point N1: 3A\n- Current through point N2: 2A\n- Voltage across A to N1: 4V\n- Voltage across N1 to N2: 4V\n- Voltage across N2 to B: 4V\n\nDetermine the circuit topology and resistor values. The circuit contains three resistors R1, R2, and R3.",
        "target_scores": {
          "Reasoning A : Looking at the measurements:\n- Total voltage A to B: 12V\n- Total current: 3A\n- Current at N1: 3A, Current at N2: 2A\n\nSince the current decreases from 3A to 2A at N2, this means 1A must branch off somewhere. This indicates a parallel configuration.\n\nVoltage drops:\n- A to N1: 4V with 3A \u2192 R1 = 4/3 = 1.33\u03a9\n- N1 to N2: 4V with 2A \u2192 R2 = 4/2 = 2\u03a9\n- N2 to B: 4V with 2A \u2192 R3 = 4/2 = 2\u03a9\n\nBut wait, we have 1A branching off at N1. This 1A must go through a parallel path from N1 to N2. Let's call this R2_parallel.\n\nFor the parallel resistor: 4V with 1A \u2192 R2_parallel = 4\u03a9\n\nTopology: A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1 -> [R2(2\u03a9) in parallel with R2_parallel(4\u03a9)] -> N2 -> R3(2\u03a9) -> B\n\nVerification: Parallel resistance = (2\u00d74)/(2+4) = 1.33\u03a9\nTotal = 1.33 + 1.33 + 2 = 4.67\u03a9\nCurrent = 12/4.67 = 2.57A \u2717 (doesn't match 3A)": 0,
          "Reasoning B: Looking at the measurements:\n- Total voltage A to B: 12V\n- Total current: 3A\n- Current through N1: 3A\n- Current through N2: 2A\n- Voltage drops: A to N1 = 4V, N1 to N2 = 4V, N2 to B = 4V\n\nKey observation: Current through N1 is 3A (same as source), but current through N2 is only 2A. This means 1A branches off between N1 and N2.\n\nLet me analyze each section:\n\nSection 1 (A to N1):\n- Voltage: 4V, Current: 3A\n- R1 = 4V / 3A = 1.33\u03a9\n\nSection 2 (N1 to N2):\n- Voltage drop: 4V\n- Current through main path to N2: 2A\n- Current that branched off: 1A\n- This suggests R2 in series carrying 2A, and R3 in parallel with R2\n- R2 = 4V / 2A = 2\u03a9 (main path)\n- For parallel: the 1A branch also sees 4V, so R3 = 4V / 1A = 4\u03a9\n- R3 connects from N1 directly to N2 in parallel\n\nSection 3 (N2 to B):\n- Wait, the current at N2 is 2A, and voltage drop N2 to B is 4V\n- This doesn't account for the parallel current rejoining\n\nLet me reconsider: The 1A that branched at N1 through R3(4\u03a9) would rejoin at N2, making current at N2 = 2A + 1A = 3A again. But measurement shows 2A at N2.\n\nActually, if current at N2 is 2A going toward B, and we had 3A at N1, then 1A must be going through a branch from N1 that doesn't pass through N2.\n\nNew interpretation: R3 branches from N1 directly to B (bypassing N2)!\n\nTopology:\n- A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1\n- N1 -> R2(2\u03a9) -> N2 -> B (but this doesn't complete)\n\nActually, looking more carefully: if voltage from A to N1 is 4V, N1 to N2 is 4V, and N2 to B is 4V, all sections drop 4V. With 3A through N1 and 2A through N2:\n\nSeries path: A -> R1 -> N1 -> R2 -> N2 -> (something) -> B\nR1 = 4V/3A = 1.33\u03a9 (all 3A goes through)\nAt N1: current splits\nR2 = 4V/2A = 2\u03a9 (only 2A continues)\nR3 in parallel from N1 to B: carries 1A\n\nFor R3: voltage from N1 to B = 4V + 4V = 8V, current = 1A\nR3 = 8V/1A = 8\u03a9\n\nBut N2 to B drops 4V with 2A, so there must be another resistor:\nR4 from N2 to B = 4V/2A = 2\u03a9\n\nWait, let me reconsider the whole structure:\n- A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1\n- From N1: \n  - Path 1: R2(2\u03a9) -> N2 -> R4(2\u03a9) -> B (carries 2A)\n  - Path 2: R3(8\u03a9) -> B (carries 1A, bypasses N2)\n\nVerification:\n- R1 = 4/3 \u03a9\n- Parallel section: R2+R4 = 4\u03a9 in parallel with R3 = 8\u03a9\n- Parallel resistance = (4\u00d78)/(4+8) = 2.67\u03a9\n- Total = 1.33 + 2.67 = 4\u03a9\n- Current = 12/4 = 3A \u2713\n- Current through series path = 12/(1.33+4) = 2.25A \u2717\n\nHmm, let me recalculate:\nActually, for parallel: V = 8V\nPath 1 (through N2): 8V / 4\u03a9 = 2A \u2713\nPath 2 (direct): 8V / 8\u03a9 = 1A \u2713\nTotal parallel current = 3A \u2713\n\nTopology (correct):\nA -> R1(4/3\u03a9) -> N1 -> [R2(2\u03a9)+R4(2\u03a9) in series] || R3(8\u03a9) -> B\nWhere N2 is between R2 and R4 on the series path": 1,
          "Reasoning C: Looking at the measurements:\n- Total voltage: 12V, Current: 3A\n- Current at N1: 3A, Current at N2: 2A\n- Voltages: A-N1: 4V, N1-N2: 4V, N2-B: 4V\n\nThe current stays constant at 3A until N1, then drops to 2A at N2. This is a series circuit with one parallel branch.\n\nCalculating resistances:\n- R1 = 4V / 3A = 1.33\u03a9 (A to N1)\n- At N1, current splits: 2A continues, 1A branches\n- R2 = 4V / 2A = 2\u03a9 (N1 to N2 on main path)\n- R3 = 4V / 2A = 2\u03a9 (N2 to B)\n\nThe 1A that branches at N1 must go through a parallel resistor. Since the voltage from N1 to N2 is 4V:\n- R_parallel = 4V / 1A = 4\u03a9\n\nThis parallel resistor connects N1 to N2, in parallel with R2.\n\nTopology: A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1 -> [R2(2\u03a9) || R_parallel(4\u03a9)] -> N2 -> R3(2\u03a9) -> B\n\nVerification:\n- Parallel resistance between N1 and N2: (2\u00d74)/(2+4) = 1.33\u03a9\n- Total resistance: 1.33 + 1.33 + 2 = 4.67\u03a9\n- Current: 12V / 4.67\u03a9 = 2.57A \u2717 (doesn't match 3A)\n\nWait, this doesn't work. Let me reconsider...\n\nActually, maybe the measurements are telling us that after N2, the currents recombine. So:\n- 3A through R1\n- At N1: splits to 2A (through R2) and 1A (through parallel path)\n- Both paths reach N2\n- From N2: recombined 3A goes through R3\n\nBut the measurement says current at N2 is 2A, not 3A. So the parallel path must bypass N2 entirely and go directly to B.\n\nR_parallel connects N1 to B directly:\n- Voltage from N1 to B: 4V + 4V = 8V\n- Current: 1A\n- R_parallel = 8\u03a9\n\nSeries path: A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1 -> R2(2\u03a9) -> N2 -> R3(2\u03a9) -> B\nParallel: N1 -> R_parallel(8\u03a9) -> B\n\nVerification:\n- Series path resistance from N1 to B: 2 + 2 = 4\u03a9\n- Parallel with 8\u03a9: (4\u00d78)/(4+8) = 2.67\u03a9  \n- Total: 1.33 + 2.67 = 4\u03a9\n- Current: 12/4 = 3A \u2713\n\nTopology: A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1, then N1 splits:\n  - Path 1: R2(2\u03a9) -> N2 -> R3(2\u03a9) -> B\n  - Path 2: R_parallel(8\u03a9) -> B": 0,
          "Reasoning D : Analyzing the measurements:\n- Total: 12V, 3A\n- Current constant through N1 (3A) and N2 (2A)\n- Voltages: 4V, 4V, 4V for each section\n\nThis is clearly a simple series circuit. The current reduction from 3A to 2A is due to measurement error or the current measurement at N2 is before a parallel branch that we're not seeing.\n\nIf we assume it's purely series:\nR1 = 4V / 3A = 1.33\u03a9\nR2 = 4V / 3A = 1.33\u03a9  \nR3 = 4V / 3A = 1.33\u03a9\n\nTotal = 4\u03a9, Current = 12/4 = 3A \u2713\n\nTopology: A -> R1(1.33\u03a9) -> N1 -> R2(1.33\u03a9) -> N2 -> R3(1.33\u03a9) -> B\n\nThe current measurement of 2A at N2 must be an error or taken at a different point.": 0,
